=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The ticket's premise is incorrect — router4 is not down and Location C is fully operational. Direct measurement inside the lab contradicts every part of the user report.

Router4's LAN-facing interface (eth0, 192.168.200.1/24, connected via sw4_r4 → switch4 → switch5/switch6) is administratively and operationally UP with the correct /24 address assigned. FRR is running RIP version 2 on that interface and the full routing table is populated: router4 holds connected routes for 192.168.200.0/24, 192.168.15.0/24, and 172.25.1.0/24, plus RIP-learned routes to all remote subnets (172.16.x, 10.x, 192.168.1.0, 192.168.100.0).

All four Location C end-hosts (PC11–PC14, addresses .11–.14) have their default gateway set to 192.168.200.1 and achieve 0% packet loss pinging it. End-to-end cross-site reachability is also confirmed: PC11 successfully pings PC4 at Location A (172.16.10.4) across three routing hops with sub-4 ms RTT.

There is no injected or spontaneous fault anywhere in the Location C path. The FRR daemon on router4 is healthy, interfaces are up, RIP convergence is complete, and the data plane is forwarding correctly.

Root cause: No fault exists — the helpdesk ticket is based on a false premise. The reporter's perception that the gateway is dead is not reflected in any live measurement.

Corrective action: No configuration change is needed. Close the ticket and advise the reporter to re-test; if they continue to experience issues, check for a local client-side misconfiguration (e.g., a static address pointing to the wrong gateway on their specific workstation).
=== END ===